            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Marks each element of an array that is one of a set of values: true where it is, false where it is not.\n\nUse it to pick out the joints, channels, notes or labels you care about from a longer list - which of these readings come from the arm joints, which notes are in the scale, which zones are active - and then select them with np.where or np.argwhere, or count them with np.sum.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.isin <set: numbers or words ...>\nDefault: no set (send one to the set inlet).\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.isin 0 4 7\nnp.isin do mi sol\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe elements to test: numbers or words, any shape. Every input is tested and sent.\n\nset:\nThe values that count, replacing any given as arguments. Held.\n\ninvert:\nAn option, under the node. On, the answer is reversed: true for elements NOT in the set.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue or false for each element, the shape of the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: which of 1 to 6 are even - in the set 2 4 6. Bottom: which of the notes do re mi fa sol are in the chord do mi sol. A metro re-sends both twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.intersect1d gives the shared values themselves. == tests against a single value. np.where and np.argwhere use the mask.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 860,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Marks each element of an array that is one of a set of values: true where it is, false where it is not.\n\nUse it to pick out the joints, channels, notes or labels you care about from a longer list - which of these readings come from the arm joints, which notes are in the scale, which zones are active - and then select them with np.where or np.argwhere, or count them with np.sum.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.isin <set: numbers or words ...>\nDefault: no set (send one to the set inlet).\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.isin 0 4 7\nnp.isin do mi sol\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe elements to test: numbers or words, any shape. Every input is tested and sent.\n\nset:\nThe values that count, replacing any given as arguments. Held.\n\ninvert:\nAn option, under the node. On, the answer is reversed: true for elements NOT in the set.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue or false for each element, the shape of the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: which of 1 to 6 are even - in the set 2 4 6. Bottom: which of the notes do re mi fa sol are in the chord do mi sol. A metro re-sends both twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.intersect1d gives the shared values themselves. == tests against a single value. np.where and np.argwhere use the mask."
        },
